---
/**
 * What leaves your Mac, and what does not.
 *
 * Written as an inventory rather than a policy, as armada's and bastion's are,
 * because for this app the honest version is short.
 *
 * CLAIM is the network claim word for word as README.md, PLAN.md §5 and
 * SECURITY.md state it, and scripts/audit-network.sh checks it. If they and this
 * page disagree, they are right and this page is stale: change all of them in one
 * commit, and move the date.
 *
 * The update check section says what Sparkle 2.9.6 sends with the Info.plist
 * apps/apple ships (SUSendProfileInfo false) and the delegate Updates.swift
 * implements (no feed parameters, no extra headers): its default User-Agent,
 * `<CFBundleName>/<CFBundleShortVersionString> Sparkle/<version>`
 * (SPUUserAgent+Private.m), and a feed URL with no query, since a parameter list
 * left empty returns the bare feed URL (SPUUpdater.m, parameterizedFeedURL).
 * Turning the profile on, or adding feed parameters, changes this page.
 *
 * Scoped to what Drakar and Sparkle add that identifies anything, never a count of
 * headers: Sparkle also sets an Accept header on the appcast request (RSS first,
 * anything else at a low q), which describes the feed format and nothing about the
 * person. The request goes through the in-process URL loading system
 * (SUEnableDownloaderService is false), which adds headers of
 * its own, Accept-Language among them, as it does for every request; the page
 * says so in general terms rather than listing them, so it stays true without a
 * capture.
 *
 * SUAllowsAutomaticUpdates is false in the plist, so Sparkle's update window
 * never offers to download and install updates automatically, and the install
 * sentence can say it always asks. `scripts/audit-network.sh` fails a bundle
 * where that key is anything else.
 *
 * The purchase section is armada's, gated on SELLING the same way.
 *
 * The feedback paragraphs are armada's: the app's Send Feedback opens /feedback/
 * and sends nothing itself, and the page posts only when you press Send.
 *
 * DIVERGES from armada, whose page also covers its MCP endpoint and voice
 * (Drakar has neither).
 */
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import {
  APP_NAME,
  DOCS,
  DOWNLOAD,
  FEEDBACK_API,
  FEEDBACK_URL,
  REPO_URL,
  SECURITY_EMAIL,
  SECURITY_EMAIL_HREF,
  SELLING,
  SITE_URL,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF,
} from "../config";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

/** Set by hand when the page changes, never `new Date()`, which shows the deploy date. */
const updated = "31 October 2026";

const h2 = "m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]";
const lead = "mt-5 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-pretty text-fg-dim";
const prose = "mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-pretty text-fg-dim";
const link = "text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-hi";
const code = "[&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[0.9em] [&_code]:break-words [&_code]:text-fg/80";
---

<Layout
  title={`Privacy · ${APP_NAME}`}
  description="What leaves your Mac and what does not: one update check you turn on yourself, no telemetry, and a licence checked offline."
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class:list={["wrap max-w-[820px] py-20", code]}>
    <div class="mono text-accent">Privacy</div>
    <h1 class="mt-3 mb-0 font-display text-[clamp(28px,4.4vw,40px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.04em]">
      Privacy
    </h1>
    <p class="mono mt-3 mb-0">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class={h2}>What Drakar claims</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        Drakar listens on one network socket, on 127.0.0.1, which answers only a client holding
        this launch's token, and its session host on one Unix socket, which only your own Drakar
        can reach. The only connections Drakar opens of its own are Sparkle's, to check for and
        fetch updates, and only once you said yes to update checks. Everything else that reaches
        the network is the <code>claude</code> or <code>codex</code> you installed.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        There is no analytics, no telemetry, no crash reporter and no first-run ping.
        <a href={DOCS.security} rel="noopener" class={link}>SECURITY.md</a> lists each part of that
        claim and how <a href={DOCS.audit} rel="noopener" class={link}>scripts/audit-network.sh</a>
        checks it, against the source, the app you downloaded, and the copy running on your Mac.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        Help › Send Feedback is not a second connection. It opens the
        <a href={FEEDBACK_URL} class={link}>feedback form</a> in your browser with the app version,
        macOS version, Mac model and language in the address bar, and the app sends nothing itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>The update check</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        It is off until you answer yes on Home or in Settings › Updates, or press Check Now there.
        Before that, Drakar never builds the updater, so it opens no connection at all.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        A check reads one file, <code>{DOWNLOAD.appcast}</code>, which redirects to the latest
        release on GitHub. With automatic checks on, that happens about once a day.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        The only thing Sparkle adds that says anything about you or your Mac is its default
        <code>User-Agent</code> header, which names the app, its version and Sparkle's version, for
        example
        <code>Drakar/1.0.0 Sparkle/2.9.6</code>. It adds no licence key and no machine id. Sparkle
        can also add a system profile with your macOS version, Mac model, processor, memory and
        language, but Drakar ships with that turned off.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        Like any request your Mac makes, the check also carries your IP address and the standard
        headers macOS sends with every web request, such as your language settings.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        If a newer release exists and you choose to install it, Sparkle downloads that release's
        <code>Drakar.zip</code> from GitHub the same way, and installs it only if it carries Drakar's
        own signature. It always asks before installing: Drakar turns off Sparkle's option to
        download and install updates automatically, so nothing is ever installed in the background.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>The programs it runs</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        A session runs the <code>claude</code> or <code>codex</code> you installed, unmodified, on
        your own sign-in. Those programs talk to Anthropic or OpenAI, and to whatever your MCP
        servers and tools reach, under those companies' terms. What they send is between you and
        them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Your licence</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        A licence key is checked on your Mac, against a public key built into the app, and the
        trial is counted on your Mac too. Drakar never calls a licence server, so an installed
        copy has no idea who bought it, and the app never sends your email anywhere.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Buying a licence</h2>
      {
        !SELLING && (
          <p class={lead}>
            Nothing is on sale yet. This is how a purchase will be handled once it is.
          </p>
        )
      }
      <p class={SELLING ? lead : prose}>
        Apart from anything you choose to write to us, this is the one place personal data exists
        at all, and it exists because a licence has to be deliverable and a refund has to be
        traceable to a sale.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        Checkout is handled by Stripe, who take the payment details; this site never sees a card
        number. What is kept afterwards is your email address, the Stripe session and payment
        identifiers, and the licence issued against them. The email is used to send you the key and
        to find the licence again if you ask for it or a refund needs it. It is not used for
        anything else, and there is no mailing list to be added to.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>This site</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        Static files on Cloudflare, with no cookies and no third-party embeds. It loads no
        fonts or images from anywhere else, and its content security policy refuses requests to
        origins it does not name. Cloudflare keeps its own request logs, as any host does.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        It counts page views with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which Cloudflare adds to every site on
        mgcrea.io. That sets no cookie and stores nothing on your device. It records the page
        address, the referring page, your browser and operating system, your screen size, how long
        the page took to load, and the country the request came from. It never receives your IP
        address, and no identifier links one visit to another or to you. Any content blocker stops
        it, and the page works the same without it.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        The download links redirect to
        <a href={`${REPO_URL}/releases`} rel="noopener" class={link}>Drakar's releases on GitHub</a>,
        and GitHub's own privacy terms cover what happens there.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        The <a href={FEEDBACK_URL} class={link}>feedback form</a> posts what you typed, and the four
        diagnostic fields shown beside it, to <code>{new URL(FEEDBACK_API).host}</code>, a small worker
        of ours. It does so only when you press Send, and keeps the report so it can be answered. The
        email address is optional and used only to reply to you. No other page on this site talks to
        it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Asking</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        Questions go to <a href={SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF} class={link}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>. To get a
        copy of what is held against your email, or to have it deleted, write there too. Deleting a
        purchase record ends the licence it belongs to, because the record is the licence. If you find
        Drakar doing something this page says it does not, that is a security report: write to
        <a href={SECURITY_EMAIL_HREF} class={link}>{SECURITY_EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        If this page changes, the date at the top moves. The canonical copy lives at
        <a href={`${SITE_URL}/privacy/`} class={link}>{SITE_URL}/privacy/</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
